# The Myth of the Twin Rivers

In ancient times, two rivers flowed from opposing mountains—the River of Silver Waters and the River of Golden Light. Each spring, the Snow Spirit would melt the peaks, filling both rivers with life-giving flow.

One year, drought struck the land. The Snow Spirit appeared to the rivers and said, "I have only enough melt for one of you to flow fully. But if you each give half your flow to the other, the joining will create a force three times as powerful, enough for both to thrive and nourish all the valleys below."

The River of Silver Waters spoke first: "I will give my flow freely, trusting in the transformation." And it poured its waters toward the other.

The River of Golden Light hesitated, fearing depletion. It held back its waters, accepting the Silver's gift but returning nothing.

That season, the Golden Light flowed alone but weakly—barely a stream. The Silver Waters ran dry. The valleys withered.

The Snow Spirit wept: "Trust multiplied becomes abundance. But when one holds back while the other gives, both worlds diminish."

Ever since, the rivers have known: shared risk creates shared prosperity, but betrayal of trust impoverishes all.
